  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1016 PM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nWV imagery showing upper high center over the Sonoran Desert.\nPolar jet has impinged somewhat toward our area but remaining north. \nWithin and under the high, recycled moisture persists over \nArizona and southern New Mexico. Satellite and KEPZ sounding show \nPWs around 1.2 inches this afternoon. PW trend for Friday and the \nweekend is to shed about another .2-.3 inches as the upper high \nmoves from far western New Mexico Friday to the lower Mississippi \nValley Sunday. This will tend to push any existing moisture \nfurther west. Thus low POPs will continue but mainly for the \nmountains and areas west of the RG Valley. Temperatures will also \nbe warmest during this period with lowland highs approaching 100 \ndegrees. \n\nMonday and Tuesday...moisture continues to increase as upper high \nallows increasing southerly flow aloft. PWs approach 1.3-1.4 inches. \nExpect rain chances all areas to increase significantly, with \nlow/moderate flood risk. Main upper high almost as far east as the \nCarolinas by Tuesday. Despite this good location for moisture \nadvection, ECMWF continues to be a bit more bearish with rain as the \nupper ridge out of the high continues to nose westward over New \nMexico. However either solution looks promising.\n\nWednesday into next weekend...upper high generally begins drifting \nwest again, likely forcing much of the moisture west toward the \nArizona line. The one interesting feature toward the end of this \nperiod is the possibility of a polar jet short wave dropping into \nNew Mexico Saturday. This would briefly bring the moisture plume \neast again across the CWA, with some severe storm potential. Still a \nlong ways out though.",
